      "parts": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "You are working in my dotfiles/environment repository:\n\n`https://github.com/MandavkarPranjal/environment`\n\nI already use tmux heavily and do NOT want to replace it with Zellij, dvtm, abduco, or another multiplexer.\n\nYour task is to optimize my existing tmux workflow for:\n\n* lower startup/session-creation latency\n* fewer unnecessary process spawns\n* fewer tmux round-trips\n* simpler and more deterministic scripts\n* preserving my existing UX and keybindings\n* preserving compatibility with Ghostty, Neovim, OpenCode, fzf, Hyprland, and my existing dotfiles\n\nDo not make broad architectural changes unrelated to tmux.\n\n### Files to inspect first\n\nRead and understand these files before modifying anything:\n\n* `home/.config/tmux/tmux.conf`\n* `home/scripts/tmux-sessionizer`\n* `home/scripts/tmux-list`\n* `home/scripts/tmux-cht.sh`\n* `home/scripts/open-repos`\n* `home/.config/hypr/bindings.lua`\n* `home/.config/hypr/bindings.conf`\n* `home/.config/ghostty/config`\n* any `.tmux-sessionizer` files in the repository\n\nAlso search the repo for:\n\n* `tmux `\n* `send-keys`\n* `new-window`\n* `new-session`\n* `display-popup`\n* `list-sessions`\n* `switch-client`\n* `attach-session`\n* `has-session`\n\nBefore editing, explain briefly what the current tmux workflow does and identify any additional tmux-dependent scripts you find.\n\n### Primary goal\n\nKeep tmux, but make the existing implementation more efficient.\n\n#### 1. Rewrite `tmux-sessionizer`\n\nThis is the highest-priority change.\n\nCurrent behavior must remain:\n\n* select a project with fzf\n* create a tmux session if it does not exist\n* start Neovim in the first window\n* create a shell window\n* create an OpenCode window running `opencode .`\n* switch to the selected session when already inside tmux\n* attach when outside tmux\n* preserve support for project-specific `.tmux-sessionizer`\n* preserve the existing project search locations\n\nImprove it by:\n\n* avoiding `pgrep tmux`\n* avoiding `tmux list-sessions | grep`\n* use `tmux has-session`\n* avoid unnecessary `tmux send-keys`\n* start commands directly through `tmux new-session` / `tmux new-window`\n* use named windows instead of arbitrary window index `69`\n* use robust shell quoting\n* avoid unnecessary subshells/processes\n* avoid race conditions around session creation\n* make the script safe with spaces in project paths\n* keep session names deterministic\n* avoid creating duplicate sessions under concurrent invocation where reasonably possible\n\nPrefer patterns such as:\n\n```bash\ntmux new-session -d -s \"$session_name\" -n nvim -c \"$project_path\" 'nvim'\ntmux new-window -d -t \"$session_name\" -n shell -c \"$project_path\"\ntmux new-window -d -t \"$session_name\" -n opencode -c \"$project_path\" 'opencode .'\n```\n\nDo not blindly copy that example; adapt it correctly to the existing project behavior.\n\nUse meaningful window names such as:\n\n* `nvim`\n* `shell`\n* `opencode`\n\nDo not preserve window number `69` just for backward compatibility unless another script actually depends on it. Search the repo first.\n\n#### 2. Improve session detection\n\nReplace patterns like:\n\n```bash\ntmux_running=$(pgrep tmux)\n```\n\nand:\n\n```bash\ntmux list-sessions | grep -q ...\n```\n\nwith native tmux commands such as:\n\n```bash\ntmux has-session -t ...\n```\n\nUse `new-session -A` where it genuinely simplifies the logic.\n\nBe careful about the difference between:\n\n* no tmux server\n* tmux server exists\n* current shell is inside tmux\n* target session already exists\n\nPreserve correct attach vs switch-client behavior.\n\n#### 3. Improve project discovery\n\nThe current sessionizer uses a large `find` command over several project roots.\n\nEvaluate replacing it with `fd` if `fd` is already used/installed by this environment.\n\nPreserve the current project roots, but:\n\n* remove duplicated/overlapping roots where appropriate\n* avoid scanning `$HOME` unnecessarily\n* avoid duplicate results\n* keep fzf behavior equivalent\n* handle missing directories gracefully\n\nDo not add a mandatory dependency without checking the existing environment first.\n\n#### 4. Make `tmux-sessionizer` popup-friendly\n\nThe current workflow launches the sessionizer as a tmux window.\n\nEvaluate changing the `prefix + f` binding from:\n\n```bash\ntmux neww tmux-sessionizer\n```\n\nto a `display-popup` workflow.\n\nThe desired behavior is:\n\n* popup opens\n* fzf runs inside popup\n* selecting a project switches/attaches to its session\n* popup disappears\n* no unnecessary temporary tmux window remains\n\nPreserve the current keybinding unless there is a strong reason not to.\n\nUse a sensible popup size.\n\nMake sure this works both:\n\n* from inside tmux\n* outside tmux\n\nIf the existing script must change to support this correctly, change it.\n\n#### 5. Simplify `tmux-list`\n\nCurrent behavior:\n\n* list sessions using fzf\n* attach outside tmux\n* switch-client inside tmux\n\nKeep that behavior.\n\nUse `tmux list-sessions -F ...` only when actually needed, and avoid external `grep` where possible.\n\nHandle the case of no tmux server / no sessions cleanly.\n\n#### 6. Simplify `tmux-cht.sh`\n\nPreserve all current functionality.\n\nWhen opening cheat.sh results in tmux:\n\n* avoid unnecessary `bash -c` where possible\n* create the window directly with the command\n* give the resulting window a useful name if appropriate\n* preserve `less -R`\n* preserve fzf preview and query behavior\n* preserve operation outside tmux\n\nDo not break URLs containing special shell characters.\n\n#### 7. Keep `open-repos` stable\n\nDo not rewrite `open-repos` just for the sake of rewriting it.\n\nIt already contains tmux-popup-specific handling.\n\nOnly make changes if they provide a concrete improvement:\n\n* correctness\n* lower process overhead\n* simpler code\n* more robust quoting\n* fewer tmux calls\n\nPreserve current behavior for:\n\n* running inside normal tmux panes\n* running inside tmux popups\n* detecting current working directory\n* choosing Git remotes\n* choosing Git web frontends\n* launching browsers outside the popup process group\n\n#### 8. Improve Hyprland tmux launcher\n\nInspect the existing binding that effectively does:\n\n```bash\ntmux attach || tmux new -s Work\n```\n\nReplace this with the cleanest native tmux equivalent, likely based on:\n\n```bash\ntmux new-session -A -s Work\n```\n\nwhile preserving:\n\n* current working directory behavior\n* `uwsm-app`\n* `xdg-terminal-exec`\n* existing keyboard shortcut\n\nDo not break the Hyprland environment.\n\n#### 9. TPM and plugin cleanup\n\nInspect the current tmux plugin setup.\n\nOnly remove TPM or inline plugin configuration if:\n\n* the plugin functionality is not actually needed, or\n* the same behavior can be retained safely without the plugin manager.\n\nDo not remove `tmux-sensible` blindly.\n\nA tiny theoretical startup improvement is not worth breaking the config.\n\nIf you decide TPM should stay, leave it alone.\n\n#### 10. Performance-sensitive tmux config review\n\nReview `home/.config/tmux/tmux.conf` specifically for actual performance issues.\n\nDo NOT add random \"tmux performance\" settings from dotfiles/blogs.\n\nKeep:\n\n* `escape-time 0`\n* extended keys / CSI-u\n* clipboard support\n* mouse\n* vi copy mode\n* current prefix\n* current workflow bindings\n\nOnly change a setting if you can explain the measurable or practical benefit.\n\n### Important compatibility requirements\n\nDo not break:\n\n* Ghostty CSI-u key handling\n* Shift+Enter / Alt+Shift+Enter behavior\n* Neovim copy/paste\n* OSC 52 clipboard support\n* fzf\n* OpenCode\n* project-specific `.tmux-sessionizer`\n* tmux popup behavior\n* session attach/switch behavior\n* current keybindings unless explicitly improved\n* Stow layout\n\nDo not introduce unnecessary dependencies.\n\n### Code quality requirements\n\nUse:\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\nset -euo pipefail\n```\n\nwhere appropriate, but do not blindly add `set -e` to scripts whose current behavior depends on commands legitimately returning non-zero.\n\nUse:\n\n* proper quoting\n* arrays for lists\n* `command -v` for optional dependencies\n* clear functions\n* meaningful variable names\n* no useless `echo | grep` pipelines\n* no command substitutions when direct shell operations are cleaner\n\nKeep scripts readable.\n\n### Testing\n\nAfter modifications:\n\n1. Run shell syntax checks:\n\n```bash\nbash -n home/scripts/tmux-sessionizer\nbash -n home/scripts/tmux-list\nbash -n home/scripts/tmux-cht.sh\nbash -n home/scripts/open-repos\n```\n\nand any other modified shell scripts.\n\n2. Validate tmux config syntax using the appropriate tmux command.\n\n3. Test or simulate:\n\n* launching tmux from outside tmux\n* launching the `Work` session\n* running sessionizer outside tmux\n* running sessionizer inside tmux\n* selecting an existing session\n* creating a new project session\n* launching nvim\n* launching OpenCode\n* opening a shell window\n* using the session list\n* opening cheat.sh\n* opening the `open-repos` popup\n* handling paths with spaces\n* handling a missing project root\n* no existing tmux server\n\n4. Do not destroy my existing live tmux sessions during testing.\n\n### Performance validation\n\nDo not claim that the changes are \"faster\" without evidence.\n\nBefore and after the sessionizer changes, compare the number of external commands / tmux invocations used during session creation.\n\nWhere practical, benchmark session creation with something like:\n\n```bash\ntime tmux ...\n```\n\nusing a temporary test session.\n\nDo not benchmark by killing my real sessions.\n\nReport:\n\n* what changed\n* why it should be faster\n* what was actually measured\n* anything that could not be measured\n\n### Final output\n\nAfter making the changes, give me:\n\n1. A concise summary of the modifications.\n2. The exact files changed.\n3. Any important behavior changes.\n4. Any compatibi